I queued a follow-up for #580, but its own full-gates build failed on one gate and I couldn't find out which. The issue stays open until the manager's verdict.

**Already done in the earlier run:** the fix is on main as `c1c3f663`. It deletes the root `Dockerfile` and adds a test that fails on any Dockerfile that would run as root. The manager merged it green on the full gates, in a batch of 41.

**This run:**
- **Backport pull requests #599–#605** (release/2.0 to 2.6): I updated each description with main's commit `c1c3f663` and the next free patch tag: v2.0.33, v2.1.26, v2.2.23, v2.3.25, v2.4.25, v2.5.26 and v2.6.14. All seven still merge cleanly onto their release branches. No tag is cut on main: v2.6.2 to v2.6.13 have all been cut on release/2.6, so the patches come from the release branches once the backports merge.
- **Queued `13d3782`**, which removes the security exception that accepted this semgrep rule until 2026-12-31. Its stated reason named #580 as what would retire it, and today's scan of main shows no such finding. Left in place, it would let a new Dockerfile that runs as root pass unnoticed. I also added a note to PROPOSALS.md that the shell-injection exception tied to #584 is now stale too, since #584 has landed.
- **The failed build:** the full-gates build of `13d3782` (pipeline 3091521879) failed on one gate. I couldn't read its log from here, so I don't know which gate or why. Main passed the same full gates with only HISTORY.md commits since, and `go test ./scripts/` (which includes the exceptions-file test) passes locally. So I expect it's unrelated to this change, but I haven't confirmed that. I queued the land anyway: the manager builds the batch again and sends this change back with the failure if it really breaks something.
- No service handlers were touched, so the trace propagation rule doesn't apply.

**Left for the next run:** if the manager merges it, post the outcome comment (including this unexplained failure) and close the issue. If it's sent back, read the failure and fix it.
